  "text": "To find the sum of all positive integers \\( n \\) such that \\( n + 2 \\) divides the product \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the divisibility condition**: We need \\( n + 2 \\) to divide \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\).\n2. **Simplify the expression**: Let \\( d = n + 2 \\). Then \\( n = d - 2 \\). Substitute \\( n = d - 2 \\) into the expression \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\):\n   \\[\n   3((d - 2) + 3)((d - 2)^2 + 9) = 3(d + 1)((d - 2)^2 + 9)\n   \\]\n3. **Expand the expression**: Expand \\( (d - 2)^2 + 9 \\):\n   \\[\n   (d - 2)^2 + 9 = d^2 - 4d + 4 + 9 = d^2 - 4d + 13\n   \\]\n   So the expression becomes:\n   \\[\n   3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13)\n   \\]\n4. **Divisibility condition**: For \\( d \\) to divide \\( 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) \\), \\( d \\) must divide \\( 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) \\). Since \\( d \\) is a factor of \\( 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) \\), we need to check the values of \\( d \\) that are factors of the expression.\n\n5. **Check possible values of \\( d \\)**: We need to check the possible values of \\( d \\) that are positive integers and factors of the expression. Since \\( d = n + 2 \\) and \\( n \\) is a positive integer, \\( d \\) must be a positive integer greater than or equal to 3 (since \\( n = d - 2 \\geq 1 \\)).\n\n   - For \\( d = 3 \\):\n     \\[\n     n = d - 2 = 3 - 2 = 1 \\quad \\text{(not a positive integer, so discard)}\n     \\]\n   - For \\( d = 4 \\):\n     \\[\n     n = d - 2 = 4 - 2 = 2 \\quad \\text{(positive integer, so check divisibility)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     3(4 + 1)(4^2 - 4 \\cdot 4 + 13) = 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 13 = 195 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 4 + 2 = 6 \\quad \\text{so} \\quad 195 \\div 6 = 32.5 \\quad \\text{(not an integer, so discard)}\n     \\]\n   - For \\( d = 5 \\):\n     \\[\n     n = d - 2 = 5 - 2 = 3 \\quad \\text{(positive integer, so check divisibility)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     3(5 + 1)(5^2 - 4 \\cdot 5 + 13) = 3 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 18 = 324 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 5 + 2 = 7 \\quad \\text{so} \\quad 324 \\div 7 = 46.2857 \\quad \\text{(not an integer, so discard)}\n     \\]\n   - For \\( d = 6 \\):\n     \\[\n     n = d - 2 = 6 - 2 = 4 \\quad \\text{(positive integer, so check divisibility)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     3(6 + 1)(6^2 - 4 \\cdot 6 + 13) = 3 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 37 = 777 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 6 + 2 = 8 \\quad \\text{so} \\quad 777 \\div 8 = 97.125 \\quad \\text{(not an integer, so discard)}\n     \\]\n   - For \\( d = 7 \\):\n     \\[\n     n = d - 2 = 7 - 2 = 5 \\quad \\text{(positive integer, so check divisibility)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     3(7 + 1)(7^2 - 4 \\cdot 7 + 13) = 3 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 48 = 1152 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 7 + 2 = 9 \\quad \\text{so} \\quad 1152 \\div 9 = 128 \\quad \\text{(integer, so valid)}\n     \\]\n   - For \\( d = 8 \\):\n     \\[\n     n = d - 2 = 8 - 2 = 6 \\quad \\text{(positive integer, so check divisibility)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     3(8 + 1)(8^2 - 4 \\cdot 8 + 13) = 3 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 57 = 1533 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 8 + 2 = 10 \\quad \\text{so} \\quad 1533 \\div 10 = 153.3 \\quad \\text{(not an integer, so discard)}\n     \\]\n   - For \\( d = 9 \\):\n     \\[\n     n = d - 2 = 9 - 2 = 7 \\quad \\text{(positive integer, so check divisibility)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     3(9 + 1)(9^2 - 4 \\cdot 9 + 13) = 3 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 68 = 2040 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 9 + 2 = 11 \\quad \\text{so} \\quad 2040 \\div 11 = 185.4545 \\quad \\text{(not an integer, so discard)}\n     \\]\n   - For \\( d = 10 \\):\n     \\[\n     n = d - 2 = 10 - 2 = 8 \\quad \\text{(positive integer, so check divisibility)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     3(10 + 1)(10^2 - 4 \\cdot 10 + 13) = 3 \\cdot 11 \\cdot 73 = 2349 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 10 + 2 = 12 \\quad \\text{so} \\quad 2349 \\div 12 = 195.75 \\quad \\text{(not an integer, so discard)}\n     \\]\n\nFrom the above checks, the only valid \\( n \\) is 7. Therefore, the sum of all positive integers \\( n \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]",
